      en: "The Goda Mountains are a mountain range in northeastern Djibouti, running approximately 40 km northwest of Djibouti City. They rise to 1,783 meters at their highest point, Mount Goda. The range captures moisture from the Indian Ocean, creating a misty microclimate that supports the Day Forest, a rare Afromontane ecosystem. The mountains are a crucial water catchment area, with several seasonal rivers originating here. They host endemic flora such as the Djibouti spurge (Euphorbia djiboutiensis). The region is a biodiversity hotspot and a key area for conservation efforts.",
