      en: "The Allols Depression is a low-lying basin in southwestern Djibouti, reaching 150 meters below sea level. It is a remnant of a ancient lake that dried up during the Holocene, leaving behind thick salt deposits. The depression spans about 20 km² and is one of the hottest places on Earth, with summer temperatures exceeding 50°C. The landscape is barren, with occasional salt-tolerant plants like Salsola. Local Afar tribes harvest salt from the pans, a trade that has been ongoing for centuries. The depression is also a key site for geological studies on rift valley formation and evaporite deposition. Due to its harsh conditions, it remains largely uninhabited except for temporary encampments.",
